# smax derivative func is broken

# Memory Efficiency: By storing only prev_a on the way forward and discarding it effectively after the update, I avoid holding onto the final network output matrix in memory twice (once in the main loop for loss calculation, once inside the model object).


Project Overview: Dynamic Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP)
- A fully custom, from-scratch Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) built in Python and NumPy to classify the FashionMNIST dataset. This project demonstrates the application of fundamentals I have learnt throughout my university courses of linear algebra, introduction to AI, and calculus. I purposely implemented this project without without relying on high-level machine learning frameworks so I can learn more about the topics first hand and build low-level memory optimizations from scratch.  
Key Technical Achievements:
Memory-Optimized Architecture: 
- Engineered a unique 1D array state system for weights and biases, avoiding NumPy's memory reallocation overhead by pre-allocating np.zeros arrays and modifying them in-place during recursive forward and backward passes.  
Algorithmic Efficiency: 
- Streamlined backpropagation calculus by calculating the gradient with respect to biases first, using it to efficiently derive the gradient with respect to weights to prevent redundant computations. Implemented a lightweight data shuffling algorithm using an index array to reduce memory consumption during batching.  
Dynamic & Configurable: 
- Highly flexible via command-line arguments, supporting dynamic hidden layer sizes, dynamic feature scaling (normalization/standardization), and interchangeable activation and loss functions.  
Smart State Caching: 
- Built a custom activation function handler that selectively caches pre-activation values only when required by functions that destroy gradients (e.g., ReLU), optimizing overall RAM usage.
Custom, Input Error Pipeline:
- Incorporated smooth error pipelining to isolate errors in user input

Instructions for use:
Please input dataset you would like to learn:\nhidden layer size(s), num classes, choice of activation func for hidden layers, activation func for outer layers, loss func, epochs, and learning rate\nEx: python3 main.py data/fashion 128 16 10 sig smax mse 100 0.1 norm\ndataset: data/fashion\nhidden layers: 128, 16\nnum classes: 10\nactivation funcs: sig (sig OR sigmoid for Sigmoid, relu for ReLU, leaky_relu for Leaky Relu, smax for Soft Max, tanh for Tanh)\nloss func: mse (mse for Mean Squared Error, bce for Binary Cross Entropy fl for Focal Loss)\nepochs: 100\nlearning rate: 0.1, norm for normalization

Notable Challenges:
- had to make sure to first create an np.zeros array that is the size for all hidden layers combined for self.model_activations in 
order to modify arrays in-place. Initially when I implemented self.model_activations, I created an empty array and planned to append
each activation layer individually to self.model_activations. HOWEVER, np arrays have immutable allocation of memory, so np.append 
returns a different array than the original one. So for the sake of memory optimization, rather than appending to it, allocated 
memory via creating np.zeros array of final array size, and created self.model_activations_idx variable to track idx when appending
to self.model_array during forward pass.
    - same thing for self.cached_z array!
    - changed np arrays of self.model_activations and self.cached_z via index slicing rather than iterating thru individual elements
- instead of using random.shuffle(dataset) to shuffle data, which creates an entirely new array, I make an lightweight array of indices
from 0 to len(dataset) - 1, [0, 1, 2, 3, ... , 59999] and then random.shuffle that array to retrieve the first batch_size indexes of that array 
to use as our samples for our batch each epoch.

AI Use:
AI was used primarily used only for undestanding concepts and minor bug fixing. It was mainly consulted for the architecture of how I should implement my MLP in an 1D setting as well as to help me wrap my head around the conceptual side of ML. i.e. Hidden Layer Architecture, the calculus of the Forward/Backward Pass, test v.s. training sets, performance measuring utilizing F1 & loss, softmax, etc.


Misc Notes for self:
- Dynamic Command-Line Args to specify model 
- Model weights and biases stored in 1D array
- Activation func method: accounts if simple activation func that is able to be undone, if not, we use caching to store previous z value.
- Loss func method: takes in specified loss function via command line and outputs appropriate loss func & its derivative to be used in backwards pass and performance evaluation
- Forward pass: implemented with recursion to go thru deeper each layer, and for loop for iterating thru each individual node in layer
- Backward pass: implmented with recursion to propagate thru layers until reach very first hidden layer, used dC/db to calculate dC/dw instead of recalculating C'a(z(w)) * a'(z(w)) since: 
    Change of Cost to biases: C = cost (aka loss), a = activ, z = value before activation b = bias
    dC/db = C'(a(z(w))) * a'(z(w)) * 1
    dC/db = C'(a(z(w))) * a'(z(w))
    Change of Cost to Weights: C = cost (aka loss), a = activ, z = value before activation w = weight 
    dC/dw = C'(a(z(w))) * a'(z(w)) * z'(w)
    dC/dw = C'(a(z(w))) * a'(z(w)) * a(prev)
    dC/dw = dC/db * a(prev)


